Republican Senator Jeff Flake believes  the GOP is no longer conservative in his  new book conscience of a conservative  the Arizona senator writes quote never  has a party so quickly or easily  abandoned its core principles as my  party did in the course of the 2016  campaign we alerts like a tranquilized  elephant from a broad consensus on  economic philosophy and free trade to an  incoherent and often untrue mash a  back-of-the-envelope popular slogans  senator platon is with us now welcome  thanks Raph me on every read from the  New York Times this morning a seemingly  in the sequence of disappointments and  blunders have rattled mr. Trump volatile  governing coalition like mr. Trump's  attacks on turn gentle session the bull  vulgar TRAI tirade buys communications  cheap and the collapse of a conservative  backed health care legislation so what  happened why is this happening well you  know being a conservative it doesn't  just mean that you adhere to  conservative policy limited government  economic freedom individual  responsibility it means you're  conservative in comportment as well your  demeanor that you you you know in a  foreign policy a conservative is nothing  if he's not sober and measured and that  matters in the White House it matters in  Congress as well and and I think we've  lost that you write erratic behavior  unmoored from principle is the opposite  of conservatives that's right you know  during the Nixon era he had what was  called the madman theory he wanted our  adversaries to think that he was a  little off kilter that might provide  some strategic advantage but there has  to be some underlying strategy beneath  that and I don't sense that we have that  now and if you just have you know  erratic behavior unmoored from principle  that's not a good combination is Donald  Trump a Republican yes he's a Republican  from your description you're saying his  behavior does not match with the  principles of the Republican Party that  you carry well I do think that he has  some things that he has done have been  conservative I think he appointed a  great Supreme Court justice regulatory  policy that he has embraced is  conservative his instincts on tax  policy I think are conservative but some  things are profoundly unconservative the  protectionist attitude our attitude  toward free trade discounting free trade  agreements trying to get out of  multinational free trade agreements the  isolationism that that's not  conservative mean we worst exactly we  need desperately to play a game of  addition and not subtraction and you  know I think this campaign the 2016  campaign was all about drilling down on  a base and not adding two numbers let's  talk about this book you kept it a  secret from your staff while you were  writing it why well this was tough to  write I mean you never want to you know  particularly in an election year for me  or election period I'm up next year you  never want to upset the applecart or  talk about unpleasant things and so it  was difficult to write I didn't want to  be talked out of it though this is too  important I think we have a crisis of  principle and we need to get back to  what conservative really means and and I  think that's the combination for the  future so I I did keep it quiet and just  tell a couple of weeks ago and but now I  hope everyone reads it I hope people in  the White House and certainly my  colleagues as well you expect to have  the support of President Trump in your  reelection bid you know that's that's up  to him obviously there are only 52  senators we have a small majority but I  think we've reached about the limits of  what we can do just with our small  majority we've got to start reaching  across the aisle and and what bothers me  I think most is that the big problems  that we have to deal with whether it's  foreign policy issues or domestic  problems like our looming debt and  deficit 20 trillion dollars in debt will  be soon adding again a trillion dollars  a year in deficit to that debt you can  only do that if both parties sit down  and share the risk and that's not going  to happen when you refer to the other  party as my enemies or losers or clowns  you can't sit down together if in fact  you had a the President had tried to  negotiate in terms of infrastructure and  I moved to healthcare would have  had a better opportunity of fashioning a  healthcare bill that would have repaired  about McAra rather than repealing it I  do think so I think we should have  started with something else keep in mind  I mean I mean from Arizona its ground  zero for the failure of the exchange  we have people paying exorbitant  premiums two hundred thousand Arizonans  will wake up today with no health care  most of them used to have it but it's  gone now because premiums are too high  and they can't buy it on the exchange so  we desperately need reforms there the  president has talked and tweeted  recently about changing the voting rules  essentially doing away with the  filibuster is that does that have  prospects of happening no and it  shouldn't that would be a big mistake we  are at our best in the Senate when we  work across the aisle I think Obama care  many of the problems that it has because  it was pushed through by one party by  the same token we've found it difficult  as one party to repeal it and so I don't  want to lurch back and forth every  couple of years from one extreme to  another the Senate the genius of the  Senate is that you know it's a saucer  that cools the milk as as George  Washington said at one point those rules  are there for a reason they're good they  force us to work across the aisle they  invite us to work across the aisle and  we're better when we do that your  dedication is to that girl I met on a  faraway beach so long ago I'm assuming  that is your wife we met on the beach on  the north shore you know why BYU has a  campus there in the first day of school  thank you for coming senator thank you  much thank you senator

